"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.8 and an ISUP grade group 2 Gleason pattern on prostatectomy. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion documented. A significant limitation is the absence of nodal sampling; therefore, pNx status cannot be definitively assessed as pN0. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows a difference in grading, with the biopsy indicating Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1) versus the prostatectomy finding of Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2).\n\nThe prognosis remains uncertain due to missing postoperative PSA data and follow-up information. Subsequent trajectory assessment would be significantly revised by obtaining a postoperative PSA level and tracking its subsequent changes over time. The current findings suggest an intermediate risk profile given the documented pathology, but the lack of nodal status widens overall uncertainty regarding local recurrence potential. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 55.09–114.42 months; its coverage has not been validated."
